The Scott "TCB" Turner writes:
-=> Some spoilers (about potions and altars) follow...

-=> Someone recently posted a spoiler about wielding potions, noting that 
-=> wielding a potion of blindess would make you blind, for instance.

That's not a feature; that's a bug.  (And not just wielding potions.)
As Ken wrote, should be fixed by Patchlevel 6.

-=> Doors are another bad addition to the game.  They add little and
-=> impose an unneeded strain on the player.  It doesn't take much effort
-=> to realize that doors would be much improved if (a) they displayed
-=> differently in different states, so that the player doesn't have to
-=> constantly shift his gaze to the message line and (b) if running into
-=> doors attempted to open them, rather than giving some stupid message.

(a) Open/closed/no doors will have different symbols in Patchlevel 6.
(b) If you could open doors by walking into them, you'd hardly be aware of
    them.  Visibly different symbols obviate most of the bother.

-=> There isn't any trick to make a game arbitrarily hard by putting in
-=> all kinds of non-intuitive or random puzzles.  Nethack is beginning to
-=> look more and more like a badly-written Adventure game, with winning
-=> being dependent on a handful of tricks that can only be discovered
-=> through random experimentation.

Such as?

What do you consider a "puzzle"?
	"How do i rob a shop?"
	"How do i get past that boulder?"
	"How do i get into the castle?"
All have many logical solutions.

The only random trick you really need know is how to level teleport
with a scroll of teleportation (unless you use a level teleport trap).

-=> But some restraint needs to be used in adding gimmicks to the game.
-=> Too many random bells and whistles detract from the basic appeal
-=> of the game - which is, apologists notwithstanding - the good old
-=> dungeon crawl, the "hjkl" keys, bumping into monsters and running
-=> down hallways.

Some found "hjkl" pretty unappealing, so we added the "number_pad" option.

